18359375, 0.0352783203125, -0.0234375, -0.02960205078125, -0.0122833251953125, -0.01171112060546875, -0.003665924072265625, 0.032958984375, 0.006633758544921875, -0.0007538795471191406, 0.0173187255859375, -0.01776123046875, -0.00894927978515625, 0.0343017578125, 0.002262115478515625, 0.033721923828125, 0.00464630126953125, 0.0086517333984375, -0.0079803466796875, 0.038055419921875, 0.011199951171875, 0.00797271728515625, 0.007785797119140625, -0.01641845703125, -0.01441192626953125, -0.0011920928955078125, -0.00972747802734375, -0.0154571533203125, 0.0374755859375, -0.0009226799011230469, 0.024871826171875, -0.037689208984375, -0.00617218017578125, -0.00612640380859375, -0.004451751708984375, 0.025146484375, -0.00982666015625, 0.006557464599609375, -0.0065765380859375, -0.0194244384765625, 0.02459716796875, -0.0203399658203125, -0.0220184326171875, 0.01485443115234375, -0.0010623931884765625, 0.015472412109375, 0.00534820556640625, 0.0357666015625, 0.0296478271484375, -0.00751495361328125, -0.007495880126953125, 0.0006284713745117188], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods\n\nStudy Design and Dataset  \nThis retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024.\n\nMulti-Agent AI Pipeline  \nA locally deployed multi-agent AI pipeline was developed for report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The pipeline consisted of three sequential agents, each with a distinct function:\n\n1. **Sectioning Agent**: Assigns sentences to anatomical sections using a combination of rule-based (regular expressions) and large language model (LLM; Llama-2-13B, default parameters, no fine-tuning) methods. Sentences with unambiguous section references are assigned via rules; ambiguous cases are resolved by the LLM using sentence context. LLM outputs were audited during development, and systematic errors led to prompt refinement or manual assignment.\n\n2. **Consistency Agent**: Checks for cross-section consistency of key findings. It identifies key clinical findings using a curated term list and flags discrepancies between Findings and Impression sections, such as mismatches in laterality, organ, or critical findings. Rules and term lists were refined using a development set of 50 reports.\n\n3. **QA Agent**: Flags gender-anatomy conflicts and checks for documentation of communication regarding critical findings. Gender-anatomy conflicts are detected via keyword matching and LLM-based contextual analysis. For critical findings, the agent checks for documentation of communication. LLM outputs were reviewed and prompt rules refined as needed.\n\nTechnical implementation details, including specific regex patterns and LLM prompt engineering, are provided in the Supplementary Materials.\n\nEvaluation Subset Selection  \nA subset of 45 reports was selected for independent evaluation by two board-certified radiologists. Stratified random sampling ensured proportional representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, undocumented critical findings) and unflagged reports, based on their prevalence in the full dataset. The subset size balanced feasibility of manual review with inclusion of sufficient examples of each error type. Limitations include reduced statistical power and generalizability due to the small sample size.\n\nEvaluation Metrics\n\n**Operational Definitions**\n- *Correctly restructured*: The report is segmented so each sentence is assigned to the appropriate anatomical section, with no alteration of clinical content or meaning. Sentences about a particular structure (e.g., \"hepatic steatosis\") appear only in the corresponding section (e.g., \"Liver\"), with no omissions, duplications, or misplacements.\n- *Incorrectly restructured*: Errors in section assignment (e.g., a lung finding in the \"Liver\" section), omitted sentences, or altered meaning (e.g., splitting a sentence so clinical context is lost). For findings relevant to multiple organs, assignment to either relevant section is acceptable; omission or assignment to an unrelated section is incorrect.\n\n**Reviewer Rubric**\n- All sentences must be present and assigned to the correct anatomical section.\n- No sentences should be assigned to multiple sections unless clinically justified (e.g., findings spanning two organs).\n- The clinical meaning of each sentence must be preserved.\n- Any deviation from these criteria constitutes an incorrect restructuring.\n\n**Examples**\n- \"There is a right lower lobe nodule\" in the \"Liver\" section: incorrect.\n- \"Mild hepatic steatosis\" in the \"Liver\" section: correct.\n- A sentence describing a finding at the interface of two organs: assignment to either section is acceptable.\n\n**QA Performance Ratings**\n- *Excellent*: All relevant issues correctly flagged, no errors or omissions.\n- *Good*: Minor errors or omissions not affecting clinical interpretation (e.g., missed typographical inconsistency).\n- *Fair*: Errors or omissions with potential to affect clinical interpretation (e.g., missing a critical finding or misclassifying a major discrepancy).\n\nReviewers received standardized instructions and a training session with example cases. For each report, reviewers independently recorded their judgments according to these definitions.\n\nReviewer Disagreement Handling  \nDisagreements were resolved by consensus discussion. If consensus could not be reached, a third board-certified radiologist adjudicated. Consensus ratings were used in all analyses.\n\nStatistical Analysis  \nReviewer agreement was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic. For key proportions (e.g., percentage of correctly restructured reports, QA performance ratings), 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated using the Wilson score interval. For QA flagging, precision, recall, and F1-score were calculated for each error type using reviewer consensus as the reference standard. The primary outcome was the proportion of reports correctly restructured by the system. Secondary outcomes included accuracy of QA flagging, precision, recall, F1-score, and inter-rater agreement. Missing or ambiguous cases were excluded from analysis and reported separately. All statistical analyses were performed using Python (v3.10) with the `scikit-learn` and `statsmodels` packages.\n\nBaseline/Comparator Data  \nManual structuring and QA performance without the AI system were not formally measured in this study. However, prior internal audits of manual reporting identified sectioning errors in approximately 10\u201315% of reports and QA documentation omissions in 5\u20138%, providing a descriptive baseline for contextualization.\n\nResults\n\n**System Performance Summary**  \nThe multi-agent AI system processed 638 reports, structuring a total of 22,270 sentences. QA issues were flagged in 90 reports (14.1%) in the full dataset. In the evaluation subset (n=45), 15 reports (33.3%) were flagged for at least one QA issue, reflecting the stratified sampling approach.\n\n| Metric                                      | Full Dataset (n=638) | Evaluation Subset (n=45) |\n|---------------------------------------------|----------------------|--------------------------|\n| Total sentences structured                  | 22,270               | 1,570                    |\n| Reports flagged for \u22651 QA issue             | 90 (14.1%)           | 15 (33.3%)               |\n| - Section mismatches                        | 80 (12.5%)           | 13 (28.9%)               |\n| - Gender-anatomy conflicts                  | 7 (1.1%)             | 2 (4.4%)                 |\n| - Undocumented critical findings            | 3 (0.5%)             | 1 (2.2%)                 |\n\n**Evaluation Subset Results**  \n- Proportion of correctly restructured reports (by reviewer consensus): 42/45 (93.3%; 95% CI: 82.1\u201398.6%)\n- QA performance ratings: Excellent in 36/45 (80.0%), Good in 7/45 (15.6%), Fair in 2/45 (4.4%)\n- Precision, recall, and F1-score for QA flagging (all error types combined): Precision 0.92, Recall 0.88, F1-score 0.90\n- Cohen\u2019s kappa for reviewer agreement: 0.89 (95% CI: 0.78\u20131.00), indicating strong agreement\n- No missing or ambiguous cases were encountered in the evaluation subset; all reports were rated by consensus.\n\n**Narrative Summary**  \nThe AI system demonstrated high accuracy in report structuring, with 93.3% of reports correctly restructured according to expert review. QA flagging showed high precision and recall, with most flagged issues corresponding to true discrepancies. Reviewer agreement was strong. The most common errors were section mismatches, followed by rare gender-anatomy conflicts and undocumented critical findings.\n\nDiscussion\n\n**Limitations**  \nThis study has several limitations. The evaluation subset was small and may not fully capture the variability of the full dataset, limiting generalizability and statistical power. The retrospective design and use of a single institution\u2019s data may introduce selection bias. The LLM component, while locally deployed and not fine-tuned, may still reflect biases present in its training data. The absence of a formal comparator group (e.g., manual structuring or QA) limits the ability to quantify improvement over baseline. Additionally, the system\u2019s performance may differ with other report types, imaging modalities, or institutions.\n\n**Future Directions**  \nFuture work should include prospective validation in larger and more diverse datasets, formal comparison with manual or alternative automated methods, and further refinement of agent logic and LLM prompts. Investigation of system performance in other clinical domains and integration with clinical workflows are also warranted.\n\n**Conclusion**  \nA locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and QA demonstrated high accuracy and reliability in a retrospective evaluation. The system effectively identified sectioning errors and QA issues, with performance comparable to or exceeding prior manual audit rates. Further validation and prospective studies are needed to confirm generalizability and clinical impact."}}